{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:42:03.180Z","ordinal":78982,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0fd4138577b17921016aea390a877881a38eea48c2eef2c87b","status":"completed","call_id":"call_662b45c714bb4caa8967b5eae6759a6d","name":"exec","input":"const r=await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,220p' docs/releases/v1.1.2.md && sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md\",workdir:\"/home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1101-integration\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:4000});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","create_time":1793734922.5311255}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:42:03.331Z","ordinal":78983,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","session_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","root_turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","response_id":"resp_0fd4138577b17921016aea39050adc81a39d8b182e61072d35","usage":{"input_tokens":171552,"cached_input_tokens":170496,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":830,"reasoning_output_tokens":731,"total_tokens":172382},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":738919718,"cached_input_tokens":734354304,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1066366,"reasoning_output_tokens":472512,"total_tokens":739986084},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":1601331447,"cached_input_tokens":1589516928,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2882554,"reasoning_output_tokens":1397683,"total_tokens":1604214001}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:42:05.212Z","ordinal":78984,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-64da4201-63df-4abd-831b-b380d761bbcf","process_id":"94112","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,220p' docs/releases/v1.1.2.md && sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1101-integration","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' docs/releases/v1.1.2.md","name":"v1.1.2.md","path":"docs/releases/v1.1.2.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md","name":"ROADMAP.md","path":"docs/v1.1.2/ROADMAP.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Nexus Code v1.1.2\n\n**Nexus Code v1.1.2** targets the full WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap that was originally planned for v1.1.0. The v1.1.0 release failed and published no release files; v1.1.1 is the published predecessor. Version numbers are immutable, so the roadmap work is assigned to v1.1.2.\n\n## Release scope and status\n\nThe intended scope is the complete [WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap](../v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md), together with any already-approved corrective changes needed for the candidate. The operation message-delivery correction described below remains relevant context for the v1.1.1 predecessor.\n\nThis document records the target scope; it does not claim that the roadmap is implemented or qualified. As of this note, roadmap implementation and v1.1.2 qualification remain incomplete; completion requires recording the applicable evidence. Work-package completion, exact-head test and type gates, independent review, release-candidate qualification, and publication are still required before v1.1.2 can be called released. Track implementation and evidence in the work-package plans and release qualification record.\n\n## Fixed\n\n### A crash-resumed unit no longer receives a multi-message prompt's later messages twice\n\nWhen a work unit's prompt carried two or more operator or inter-unit messages, and Nexus stopped after submitting that prompt but before the unit's result was saved, resume recognised only the **first** message as already submitted. Every later message from that prompt was injected into the unit's conversation again.\n\nResume now recognises each message in a submitted prompt's messages block, whatever its position. Matching is still limited to prompts that Nexus issued (`isSubmittedUnitPrompt`, from v1.1.1), so marker text in model output, tool output, or files cannot suppress a message.\n\n- Impact in v1.1.1: the duplicated message was advisory context only. No message was lost, and no `TurnResult`, verification, or acceptance outcome was affected.\n- Regression coverage: the post-prompt crash test in `cli/test/operation_release_review.test.ts` now carries two messages. It asserts that each message appears once in the durable history and is acknowledged once.\n\n## Correction to the v1.1.1 notes\n\nThe v1.1.1 notes say that a recipient receives undelivered messages \"at most once\". That holds for prompts carrying a single message. For prompts carrying several messages, v1.1.1 could re-inject the second and later messages after a post-submission crash, as described above. v1.1.2 makes the statement hold in both cases. `docs/releases/v1.1.1.md` carries a matching addendum.\n\n## Runtime requirements\n\nUnchanged from v1.1.1: Linux x86-64 with glibc 2.35 or newer, Node.js 22 or newer, and an OpenAI-compatible inference endpoint (local or an explicitly selected provider). The archive does not include Node.js. Rust and Cargo are not required at runtime.\n\n## Upgrade\n\nOperation records are unchanged: operation schema version 2, as in v1.1.1. No migration runs, and v1.1.1 can read records written by v1.1.2.\n\n## Qualification\n\nThe multi-message resume correction is present in the candidate branch (`c0afc44c`). It is one corrective change within the larger roadmap scope, not the definition of this release. Qualification must cover the complete WP-1101–WP-1108 scope on the exact final candidate, including its full test and type gates, package and upgrade checks, independent review, and installed-candidate acceptance. v1.1.1 qualification or evidence from an earlier candidate does not qualify the final v1.1.2 candidate.\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Roadmap: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Milestone:** Nexus Code `v1.1.2`  \n**Status:** In Progress  \n**Owner:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-ROADMAP-V112`  \n**Base Release:** `v1.1.1` (`b9dd0bcc`) / `v1.1.2-rc.1` (`c3e26103`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Roadmap Overview & Commitments\n\nThe v1.1.2 release line encompasses two complementary tiers:\n1. **Tier 1 (Completed in v1.1.2-rc.1):** Operational defect fix for multi-message prompt deduplication upon crash-resume ([PR #377](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/377)) and release documentation addendum.\n2. **Tier 2 (v1.1.2 Feature Enhancement):** Bounded specialist agent contracts, objective independent review boundaries (eliminating implementer confirmation bias), deterministic micro-routing (cut from PR #396), and telemetry-aware scheduling.\n\n### Status in PR #396 (owner decision: connect the new modules or cut them)\n- **Item 1, connected.** The specialist contracts are runtime roles in `cli/src/core/roles.ts`. An operation unit or `/team` target can run as one. The agent offers only the contract's permitted tools, and it refuses any other call when the call is dispatched (`roleToolAllowList`). That holds whether or not the role is read-only.\n- **Item 2, connected.** Units with the `reviewer` or `acceptance_reviewer` role run behind the boundary:\n  - Their prompt is the evidence package: the candidate diff against the operation's base commit, and the verifier receipt of the unit they depend on. Prerequisite summaries are withheld.\n  - The unit completes only on a well-formed `ACCEPT` that survives the deterministic gates. Prose, a malformed verdict, a reported defect or a failed verifier blocks the unit for an operator decision.\n  - The verifier receipt comes from the dependency's integration receipt (isolated operations) or, where nothing was integrated (for example `/team`), from the verification recorded on the dependency's completed result. A verification that is not a passing run that executed tests reaches the package as a non-zero exit code. A large (truncated) diff with no passing verifier evidence is rejected.\n  - Retrying a rejected review resumes the reviewer's conversation. A review unit or read-only specialist (`reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`, `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `security_reviewer`; read-only by construction) does not inherit the write-verification obligation that recovery adds for writer units, since it can neither have written nor run a verifier; writer units, `failure_debugger` (which can run commands) and the read-only `researcher` keep the fail-closed obligation.\n  - The base a review diffs against is the isolated operation's base commit or, for a non-isolated operation in a Git checkout, the HEAD recorded when the operation first ran (`startCommit`), so a change a unit committed is still in the diff. The base is recorded only before any unit has started; an operation persisted without one, whose work already ran, is never given a base afterwards, and its review blocks. A persisted base is resolved with `git rev-parse --verify --end-of-options` before use.\n  - `security_reviewer` is deliberately not behind the boundary: only `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` are. It is a read-only specialist whose contract lists `unified_diff` as an input, and it keeps the ordinary prompt.\n  - An empty candidate diff is treated as a failure to collect evidence (there is nothing to judge), and the worst verification across all dependencies decides the verifier exit code.\n  - If the candidate diff cannot be collected (no Git checkout, or the diff exceeds the 4 MiB buffer), the review is blocked before any model turn. That failure is never presented to the reviewer as evidence.\n  - The runtime package names the real operation id and declares no file scope: no operation or unit declares one, so the scope gate (`detectScopeViolations`) applies only to a package that supplies `authorizedScope`.\n  - The boundary complements `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`, which decides whether a verifier pass is independent evidence. It does not replace it.\n- **Items 3 and 4, cut.** Nothing in the runtime routed on the micro-router. The escalation machine duplicated the non-progress recovery in PR #398, which is connected.\n- **`TokenBudgetTracker`, cut.** It duplicated `UsageLedger` (`cli/src/usage.ts`), which already enforces `NEXUS_CLOUD_MAX_TOKENS` with in-flight reservations.\n- **Integrated with PR #398.** The recovery context's \"Permitted next actions\" lists only actions the agent's role and mode allow (`renderRecoveryContext`'s `permits` predicate).\n- **Item 5 and the CERI and comparative harness entry points are not yet connected.** The throttle needs request latency from `cli/src/client.ts`. CERI needs a `nexus eval` command in `cli/src/index.ts`. Both files are claimed by other open work.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workstream Roadmap Specifications\n\n### Item 1: Bounded Specialist Agent Profiles (Workstream A)\n- **Objective:** Introduce typed specialist profiles with strict tool permissions, input/output contracts, and token budget bounds.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `ROLE_PROFILES` has 5 untyped text roles. No structured JSON schemas, no granular tool whitelisting beyond binary read-only mode.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts`.\n  - Introduce `SpecialistContract`: permitted tools, input requirements, structured output schema, max context tokens, and escalation thresholds.\n  - Add specialist roles: `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `failure_debugger`, `security_reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/roles.ts`, `cli/src/core/tool_groups.ts`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — foundational.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Specialist units reject tools not in their declared profile with actionable errors.\n  - Specialist units emit structured, validated output artifacts.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/specialist_profiles.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Purely additive; existing 5 roles remain backward-compatible defaults.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 2: Objective Independent Review Boundary (Workstream B)\n- **Objective:** Eliminate reviewer confirmation bias by constructing an objective evidence package stripped of the implementer's narrative claims, enforcing structured review findings.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Reviewer units currently receive the implementer's summary narrative (`Completed prerequisite work: - <title>: <summary>`), introducing confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts`.\n  - Implement `buildReviewEvidencePackage`: extracts objective task requirements, authorized scope, base commit, unified diff, raw verifier receipts, and deliberately omits implementer turn narratives.\n  - Enforce structured review verdict schema:\n    ```ts\n    interface IndependentReviewVerdict {\n      verdict: \"ACCEPT\" | \"REJECT\";\n      verifiedObservations: string[];\n      suspectedDefects: Array<{ path: string; line?: number; description: string }>;\n      unprovenCriteria: string[];\n      scopeViolations: string[];\n    }\n    ```\n  - Disallow model-generated metrics from establishing authoritative `VERIFIED` status without deterministic verifier evidence.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (`SpecialistContract`), `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Critical (P0) — addresses core verification integrity.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Reviewer prompt contains zero sentences authored by the implementer.\n  - Reviewer output parse failure fails-closed as `REJECT`.\n  - Scope violations in diff are flagged deterministically.\n- **Verification Method:** Integration tests in `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Guarded by operation policy flag `policy.enforceIndependentReviewBoundary`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 3: Deterministic Micro-Routing Engine (Workstream C) — cut from PR #396\n- **Objective:** Eliminate unnecessary LLM turns for non-semantic, deterministic commands and routine file/test operations.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** All in-turn tool calls are routed through the LLM. No classification for deterministic fast-paths.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`.\n  - Implement rule-based intent matching for pure queries (test execution, symbol search, file reading).\n  - Constrain model-assisted routing with fallback to deterministic defaults.\n- **Dependencies:** None.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Deterministic queries bypass inference turns with zero latency regression.\n  - Ambiguous queries fall back gracefully to model classification with full provenance logging.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Configurable via `policy.deterministicRouting: boolean`.\n- **Complexity:** Low-Medium (2 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Workstream E) — cut from PR #396; superseded by PR #398 non-progress recovery\n- **Objective:** Replace naive retry loops with an observable escalation state machine that detects repeat failures, engages failure investigators, and escalates to deep reasoning models before blocking for operator input.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Units retry naively up to `maxRunsPerUnit` on the same resource without diagnostic enrichment.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - State transitions: `INITIAL_ATTEMPT` -> `DIAGNOSTIC_ANALYSIS` -> `DEEP_REASONING_ESCALATION` -> `OPERATOR_DECISION`.\n  - Loop detection: identical error signatures trigger immediate escalation rather than burning budget.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (Specialist Profiles).\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Repeated identical test failures trigger diagnostic specialist after 1 retry.\n  - Escalation respects model capability requirements and token bounds.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/escalation_machine.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Additive state metadata; backward compatible with v2 schema.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle (Workstream F)\n- **Objective:** Dynamically adjust concurrent execution slots based on worker queue wait, TTFT, and latency telemetry.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Static integer caps (`policy.maxConcurrentUnits`, `resource.maxConcurrentUnits`).\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Extend `ResourceRegistry` with rolling latency tracking.\n  - Throttle concurrency when latency spikes > 2x baseline, recovering on normalization.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/resources.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Low-Medium (P3).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Worker overload dynamically reduces active concurrency to prevent timeout cascades.\n- **Verification Method:** Simulation tests in `cli/test/adaptive_concurrency.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Can be disabled via `policy.adaptiveThrottle: false`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 6: Comparative Evaluation Benchmark Harness (Workstream G)\n- **Objective:** Measure and contrast Configuration A (Baseline) vs Configuration B (Specialist + Independent Review) vs Configuration C (Adaptive).\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Individual tests exist, but no unified comparative benchmark measuring token efficiency, defect escape rate, and confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `eval/comparative_orchestration_harness.ts` / `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts`.\n  - Execute standard suites with telemetry recording.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1, 2, 3.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Produces structured comparative CSV/JSON metrics across identical benchmark tasks.\n- **Verification Method:** Benchmark dry-run on synthetic tasks.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (4 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Complete in `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts` (Commit `ad8ea909`).\n\n---\n\n### Item 7: Continuous Evaluation and Regression Intelligence (CERI)\n- **Objective:** Establish a continuous, evidence-based mechanism for detecting, investigating, classifying, and reporting degradation in Nexus's end-to-end engineering capabilities, enforcing that internal evaluations may veto releases but never certify correctness without independent validation.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `eval/run.mjs` and `cli/src/evaluator.ts` execute tasks and capture candidate receipts, but lack versioned evaluation definitions, run manifests with full model/hardware provenance, designated baseline comparison, 12-state comparative classification, regression intelligence with epistemological distinction, and non-self-approving remediation pathways.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Workstream A: Versioned evaluation definitions and run manifests capturing immutable commit, dirty status, model identity, toolchain, and host environment.\n  - Workstream B: Append-only evidence persistence with atomic writes, secret redaction, and strict separation between tamper-evidence and independent validation receipts.\n  - Workstream C: Baseline comparator with eligibility validation and 12-state classification (`PASS`, `FAIL`, `REGRESSION_CANDIDATE`, `CONFIRMED_REGRESSION`, `IMPROVEMENT_CANDIDATE`, `FLAKY`, `INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE`, `EVALUATOR_DEFECT`, `INCONCLUSIVE`, `NOT_EXECUTED`, `CANCELLED`, `INCOMPARABLE`).\n  - Workstream D: Repeatability and flakiness analysis with bounded rerun policies.\n  - Workstream E: Capacity-aware scheduling for local dual-worker Intel Arc Pro environments.\n  - Workstream F: Representative end-to-end engineering evaluation benchmarks.\n  - Workstream G: Regression intelligence structuring findings into observation, inference, hypothesis, confirmed finding, and unresolved uncertainty.\n  - Workstream H: Reviewable remediation proposals with non-self-approval boundary.\n  - Workstream I: Structured machine-readable operational reporting with measurement limitations and blockers.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1-6, `CandidateTracker`, `ValidationReceipt`, `isolate.mjs`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — Release qualification gate.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Full provenance captured in run manifests without fabricating missing metadata.\n  - Incomparable baselines rejected explicitly rather than silently compared.\n  - Binary pass/fail forcing eliminated in favor of 12-state classification.\n  - Internal evaluations cannot mark candidates as verified without deterministic verifier receipts.\n  - Remediation proposals enforce `approvalStatus: \"PENDING_REVIEW\"`.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit and integration test suites in `cli/test/ceri_*.test.ts`.\n- **Operational Risks:** False regression alarms on non-comparable environments; benchmark overfitting; token consumption during reruns.\n- **Rollback Approach:** Purely additive modules in `cli/src/ceri/`; gated by config flag `ceri.enabled`.\n- **Complexity:** High (5 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Foundational primitives and libraries implemented in PR #396 as standalone modules, covered by unit tests only and not yet used by the agent runtime (contracts, tamper-evident evidence store, baseline comparator, epistemological investigation, non-self-approving remediation, operational reporting, and comparative evaluation framework). Interactive agent loop integration and live dual-worker GPU scheduling are scheduled for subsequent integration PRs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deferred Scope (Targeted for v1.2)\n\nThe following items are explicitly **deferred** to release v1.2:\n1. **Hosted / Multi-Tenant Concurrency:** Nexus trust boundary remains local single-operator.\n2. **Autonomous Tool Generation:** Dynamic creation of new tool definitions by models without human oversight.\n3. **Automatic PR Publication (`git.publish`):** Requires operation-level egress and policy controls.\n4. **Interactive TUI Multi-Agent Pane:** Requires terminal PTY driver redesign.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":683607511}},"started_at_ms":1793734924528,"completed_at_ms":1793734925212}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:42:06.519Z","ordinal":78985,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1a2ee-e0b6-7f11-8af0-97d08bd29637","call_id":"call_662b45c714bb4caa8967b5eae6759a6d","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 4915)\nTotal output lines: 224\n\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2\n\n**Nexus Code v1.1.2** targets the full WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap that was originally planned for v1.1.0. The v1.1.0 release failed and published no release files; v1.1.1 is the published predecessor. Version numbers are immutable, so the roadmap work is assigned to v1.1.2.\n\n## Release scope and status\n\nThe intended scope is the complete [WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap](../v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md), together with any already-approved corrective changes needed for the candidate. The operation message-delivery correction described below remains relevant context for the v1.1.1 predecessor.\n\nThis document records the target scope; it does not claim that the roadmap is implemented or qualified. As of this note, roadmap implementation and v1.1.2 qualification remain incomplete; completion requires recording the applicable evidence. Work-package completion, exact-head test and type gates, independent review, release-candidate qualification, and publication are still required before v1.1.2 can be called released. Track implementation and evidence in the work-package plans and release qualification record.\n\n## Fixed\n\n### A crash-resumed unit no longer receives a multi-message prompt's later messages twice\n\nWhen a work unit's prompt carried two or more operator or inter-unit messages, and Nexus stopped after submitting that prompt but before the unit's result was saved, resume recognised only the **first** message as already submitted. Every later message from that prompt was injected into the unit's conversation again.\n\nResume now recognises each message in a submitted prompt's messages block, whatever its position. Matching is still limited to prompts that Nexus issued (`isSubmittedUnitPrompt`, from v1.1.1), so marker text in model output, tool output, or files cannot suppress a message.\n\n- Impact in v1.1.1: the duplicated message was advisory context only. No message was lost, and no `TurnResult`, verification, or acceptance outcome was affected.\n- Regression coverage: the post-prompt crash test in `cli/test/operation_release_review.test.ts` now carries two messages. It asserts that each message appears once in the durable history and is acknowledged once.\n\n## Correction to the v1.1.1 notes\n\nThe v1.1.1 notes say that a recipient receives undelivered messages \"at most once\". That holds for prompts carrying a single message. For prompts carrying several messages, v1.1.1 could re-inject the second and later messages after a post-submission crash, as described above. v1.1.2 makes the statement hold in both cases. `docs/releases/v1.1.1.md` carries a matching addendum.\n\n## Runtime requirements\n\nUnchanged from v1.1.1: Linux x86-64 with glibc 2.35 or newer, Node.js 22 or newer, and an OpenAI-compatible inference endpoint (local or an explicitly selected provider). The archive does not include Node.js. Rust and Cargo are not required at runtime.\n\n## Upgrade\n\nOperation records are unchanged: operation schema version 2, as in v1.1.1. No migration runs, and v1.1.1 can read records written by v1.1.2.\n\n## Qualification\n\nThe multi-message resume correction is present in the candidate branch (`c0afc44c`). It is one corrective change within the larger roadmap scope, not the definition of this release. Qualification must cover the complete WP-1101–WP-1108 scope on the exact final candidate, including its full test and type gates, package and upgrade checks, independent review, and installed-candidate acceptance. v1.1.1 qualification or evidence from an earlier candidate does not qualify the final v1.1.2 candidate.\n# Nexus Code v1.1.2 Roadmap: Specialist Orchestration & Independent Review\n\n**Milestone:** Nexus Code `v1.1.2`  \n**Status:** In Progress  \n**Owner:** Principal Software Architect & Senior Implementation Engineer  \n**Document ID:** `NEXUS-ROADMAP-V112`  \n**Base Release:** `v1.1.1` (`b9dd0bcc`) / `v1.1.2-rc.1` (`c3e26103`)  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Roadmap Overview & Commitments\n\nThe v1.1.2 release line encompasses two complementary tiers:\n1. **Tier 1 (Completed in v1.1.2-rc.1):** Operational defect fix for multi-message prompt deduplication upon crash-resume ([PR #377](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/377)) and release documentation addendum.\n2. **Tier 2 (v1.1.2 Feature Enhancement):** Bounded specialist agent contracts, objective independent review boundaries (eliminating implementer confirmation bias), deterministic micro-routing (cut from PR #396), and telemetry-aware scheduling.\n\n### Status in PR #396 (owner decision: connect the new modules or cut them)\n- **Item 1, connected.** The specialist contracts are runtime roles in `cli/src/core/roles.ts`. An operation unit or `/team` target can run as one. The agent offers only the contract's permitted tools, and it refuses any other call when the call is dispatched (`roleToolAllowList`). That holds whether or not the role is read-only.\n- **Item 2, connected.** Units with the `reviewer` or `acceptance_reviewer` role run behind the boundary:\n  - Their prompt is the evidence package: the candidate diff against the operation's base commit, and the verifier receipt of the unit they depend on. Prerequisite summaries are withheld.\n  - The unit completes only on a well-formed `ACCEPT` that survives the deterministic gates. Prose, a malformed verdict, a reported defect or a failed verifier blocks the unit for an operator decision.\n  - The verifier receipt comes from the dependency's integration receipt (isolated operations) or, where nothing was integrated (for example `/team`), from the verification recorded on the dependency's completed result. A verification that is not a passing run that executed tests reaches the package as a non-zero exit code. A large (truncated) diff with no passing verifier evidence is rejected.\n  - Retrying a rejected review resumes the reviewer's conversation. A review unit or read-only specialist (`reviewer`, `acceptance_reviewer`, `semantic_explorer`, `call_site_analyzer`, `security_reviewer`; read-only by construction) does not inherit the write-verification obligation that recovery adds for writer units, since it can neither have written nor run a verifier; writer units, `failure_debugger` (which can run commands) and the read-only `researcher` keep the fail-closed obligation.\n  - The base a review diffs against is the isolated operation's base commit or, for a non-isolated operation in a Git checkout, the HEAD recorded when the operation first ran (`startCommit`), so a change a unit committed is still in the diff. The base is recorded only before any unit has started; an operation persisted without one, whose work already ran, is never given a base afterwards, and its review blocks. A persisted base is resolved with `git rev-parse --verify --end-of-options` before use.\n  - `security_reviewer` is deliberately not behind the boundary: only `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` are. It is a read-only specialist whose contract lists `unified_diff` as an input, and it keeps the ordinary prompt.\n  - An empty candidate diff is treated as a failure to collect evidence (there is nothing to judge), and the worst verification across all dependencies decides the verifier exit code.\n  - If the candidate diff cannot be collected (no Git checkout, or the diff exceeds the 4 MiB buffer), the review is blocked before any model turn. That failure is never presented to the reviewer as evidence.\n  - The runtime package names the real operation id and declares no file scope: no operation or unit declares one, so the scope gate (`detectScopeViolations`) applies only to a package that supplies `authorizedScope`.\n  - The boundary complements `cli/src/core/independent_check.ts`, which decides whether a verifier pass is independent evidence. It does not replace it.\n- **Items 3 and 4, cut.** Nothing in the runtime routed on the micro-router. The escalation machine duplicated the non-progress recovery in PR #398, which is connected.\n- **`TokenBudgetTracker`, cut.** It duplicated `UsageLedger` (`cli/src/usage…915 tokens truncated… — cut from PR #396\n- **Objective:** Eliminate unnecessary LLM turns for non-semantic, deterministic commands and routine file/test operations.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** All in-turn tool calls are routed through the LLM. No classification for deterministic fast-paths.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `cli/src/core/micro_router.ts`.\n  - Implement rule-based intent matching for pure queries (test execution, symbol search, file reading).\n  - Constrain model-assisted routing with fallback to deterministic defaults.\n- **Dependencies:** None.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Deterministic queries bypass inference turns with zero latency regression.\n  - Ambiguous queries fall back gracefully to model classification with full provenance logging.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/micro_router.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Configurable via `policy.deterministicRouting: boolean`.\n- **Complexity:** Low-Medium (2 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 4: Multi-Stage Escalation State Machine (Workstream E) — cut from PR #396; superseded by PR #398 non-progress recovery\n- **Objective:** Replace naive retry loops with an observable escalation state machine that detects repeat failures, engages failure investigators, and escalates to deep reasoning models before blocking for operator input.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Units retry naively up to `maxRunsPerUnit` on the same resource without diagnostic enrichment.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - State transitions: `INITIAL_ATTEMPT` -> `DIAGNOSTIC_ANALYSIS` -> `DEEP_REASONING_ESCALATION` -> `OPERATOR_DECISION`.\n  - Loop detection: identical error signatures trigger immediate escalation rather than burning budget.\n- **Dependencies:** Item 1 (Specialist Profiles).\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Repeated identical test failures trigger diagnostic specialist after 1 retry.\n  - Escalation respects model capability requirements and token bounds.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit tests in `cli/test/escalation_machine.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Additive state metadata; backward compatible with v2 schema.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 5: Telemetry-Informed Concurrency Throttle (Workstream F)\n- **Objective:** Dynamically adjust concurrent execution slots based on worker queue wait, TTFT, and latency telemetry.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Static integer caps (`policy.maxConcurrentUnits`, `resource.maxConcurrentUnits`).\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Extend `ResourceRegistry` with rolling latency tracking.\n  - Throttle concurrency when latency spikes > 2x baseline, recovering on normalization.\n- **Dependencies:** `cli/src/core/resources.ts`.\n- **Priority:** Low-Medium (P3).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Worker overload dynamically reduces active concurrency to prevent timeout cascades.\n- **Verification Method:** Simulation tests in `cli/test/adaptive_concurrency.test.ts`.\n- **Rollback Considerations:** Can be disabled via `policy.adaptiveThrottle: false`.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (3 days).\n\n---\n\n### Item 6: Comparative Evaluation Benchmark Harness (Workstream G)\n- **Objective:** Measure and contrast Configuration A (Baseline) vs Configuration B (Specialist + Independent Review) vs Configuration C (Adaptive).\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** Individual tests exist, but no unified comparative benchmark measuring token efficiency, defect escape rate, and confirmation bias.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Create `eval/comparative_orchestration_harness.ts` / `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts`.\n  - Execute standard suites with telemetry recording.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1, 2, 3.\n- **Priority:** Medium (P2).\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Produces structured comparative CSV/JSON metrics across identical benchmark tasks.\n- **Verification Method:** Benchmark dry-run on synthetic tasks.\n- **Complexity:** Medium (4 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Complete in `cli/src/eval/comparative_harness.ts` (Commit `ad8ea909`).\n\n---\n\n### Item 7: Continuous Evaluation and Regression Intelligence (CERI)\n- **Objective:** Establish a continuous, evidence-based mechanism for detecting, investigating, classifying, and reporting degradation in Nexus's end-to-end engineering capabilities, enforcing that internal evaluations may veto releases but never certify correctness without independent validation.\n- **Existing Capability / Gap:** `eval/run.mjs` and `cli/src/evaluator.ts` execute tasks and capture candidate receipts, but lack versioned evaluation definitions, run manifests with full model/hardware provenance, designated baseline comparison, 12-state comparative classification, regression intelligence with epistemological distinction, and non-self-approving remediation pathways.\n- **Planned Implementation:**\n  - Workstream A: Versioned evaluation definitions and run manifests capturing immutable commit, dirty status, model identity, toolchain, and host environment.\n  - Workstream B: Append-only evidence persistence with atomic writes, secret redaction, and strict separation between tamper-evidence and independent validation receipts.\n  - Workstream C: Baseline comparator with eligibility validation and 12-state classification (`PASS`, `FAIL`, `REGRESSION_CANDIDATE`, `CONFIRMED_REGRESSION`, `IMPROVEMENT_CANDIDATE`, `FLAKY`, `INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE`, `EVALUATOR_DEFECT`, `INCONCLUSIVE`, `NOT_EXECUTED`, `CANCELLED`, `INCOMPARABLE`).\n  - Workstream D: Repeatability and flakiness analysis with bounded rerun policies.\n  - Workstream E: Capacity-aware scheduling for local dual-worker Intel Arc Pro environments.\n  - Workstream F: Representative end-to-end engineering evaluation benchmarks.\n  - Workstream G: Regression intelligence structuring findings into observation, inference, hypothesis, confirmed finding, and unresolved uncertainty.\n  - Workstream H: Reviewable remediation proposals with non-self-approval boundary.\n  - Workstream I: Structured machine-readable operational reporting with measurement limitations and blockers.\n- **Dependencies:** Items 1-6, `CandidateTracker`, `ValidationReceipt`, `isolate.mjs`.\n- **Priority:** High (P1) — Release qualification gate.\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - Full provenance captured in run manifests without fabricating missing metadata.\n  - Incomparable baselines rejected explicitly rather than silently compared.\n  - Binary pass/fail forcing eliminated in favor of 12-state classification.\n  - Internal evaluations cannot mark candidates as verified without deterministic verifier receipts.\n  - Remediation proposals enforce `approvalStatus: \"PENDING_REVIEW\"`.\n- **Verification Method:** Unit and integration test suites in `cli/test/ceri_*.test.ts`.\n- **Operational Risks:** False regression alarms on non-comparable environments; benchmark overfitting; token consumption during reruns.\n- **Rollback Approach:** Purely additive modules in `cli/src/ceri/`; gated by config flag `ceri.enabled`.\n- **Complexity:** High (5 days).\n- **Implementation Status:** Foundational primitives and libraries implemented in PR #396 as standalone modules, covered by unit tests only and not yet used by the agent runtime (contracts, tamper-evident evidence store, baseline comparator, epistemological investigation, non-self-approving remediation, operational reporting, and comparative evaluation framework). Interactive agent loop integration and live dual-worker GPU scheduling are scheduled for subsequent integration PRs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deferred Scope (Targeted for v1.2)\n\nThe following items are explicitly **deferred** to release v1.2:\n1. **Hosted / Multi-Tenant Concurrency:** Nexus trust boundary remains local single-operator.\n2. **Autonomous Tool Generation:** Dynamic creation of new tool definitions by models without human oversight.\n3. **Automatic PR Publication (`git.publish`):** Requires operation-level egress and policy controls.\n4. **Interactive TUI Multi-Agent Pane:** Requires terminal PTY driver redesign.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","create_time":1793734926.5189414}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
