{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:53:26.859Z","ordinal":79580,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-5dac96b6-2189-49e0-a87e-b3c18997bbdd","process_id":"39881","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,220p' .entire/agent-guide.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.1.0/CONCURRENCY.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1101-integration","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' .entire/agent-guide.md","name":"agent-guide.md","path":".entire/agent-guide.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.1.0/CONCURRENCY.md","name":"CONCURRENCY.md","path":"docs/v1.1.0/CONCURRENCY.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"failed","aggregated_output":"sed: can't read .entire/agent-guide.md: No such file or directory\nsed: can't read docs/v1.1.0/CONCURRENCY.md: No such file or directory\n","exit_code":2,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":313941031}},"started_at_ms":1793735605539,"completed_at_ms":1793735606854}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:53:27.033Z","ordinal":79581,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-e3aa7c4e-0191-4fa0-b70f-16cdf22c869d","process_id":"51778","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git diff --check && git diff --stat && sed -n '1,180p' docs/v1.1.0/WP-1107-PLAN.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1101-integration","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git diff --check && git diff --stat && sed -n '1,180p' docs/v1.1.0/WP-1107-PLAN.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":" docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md | 11 ++++++-----\n 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)\n# WP-1107 — Provider-Constrained Tool Grammar\n\n## Specification\n\n**User story:** As a Nexus user running a supported local inference engine, I need registered tool arguments constrained by that engine so tool calls arrive as valid JSON and require fewer syntax repairs.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n\n- **AC1:** An identified llama.cpp chat endpoint receives the grammar generated from the active allowlisted tool schemas. Test: `client sends llama.cpp GBNF for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC2:** An identified vLLM endpoint receives the provider's supported JSON-schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends vLLM structured tool schema for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC3:** An identified Ollama endpoint receives its supported schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends Ollama JSON schema format for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC4:** Unknown and unsupported endpoints retain their current request shape; no grammar field is sent without positive provider identification. Test: `client omits constrained fields for unknown and unsupported providers`.\n- **AC5:** On each supported endpoint, the exact installed candidate completes 200 attempts: 50 for each of four fixed allowlisted tool schemas. Each attempt requests exactly one tool call. `native_valid` means the raw provider argument string parses with `JSON.parse` before any normalization or repair; `repaired` means raw parsing fails and the existing tool-call repair path produces parseable JSON; `invalid` includes every other outcome, including provider errors, timeouts, missing calls, and unparseable arguments. An endpoint passes only when `native_valid * 1000 >= attempts * 995` and `invalid == 0`; report exact integer counts and do not round percentages for the decision. Tests/evidence: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`; live `WP-1107 supported-endpoint syntax matrix`.\n\n**Out of scope:** Changing tool schemas or the allowlist; applying constraints to non-tool completions; measuring task-solving quality; enabling constraints on an endpoint that does not positively identify itself.\n\n**Dependencies:** Existing schema-derived grammar generator and `toolCallSyntax` telemetry; source implementation is otherwise independent. AC5 depends on an approved shared inference window and the exact candidate package.\n\n**Scope reconciliation:** The user selected the full failed v1.1.0 WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap for v1.1.2. This explicit scope supersedes the older deferral in `ROADMAP-REVIEW.md` §2.5. It does not waive the required provider references, test gates, review, or G1.1-4 endpoint evidence.\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n**Chunk 1 — Provider request adapter**\n\n- Add a typed adapter mapping positively identified `llamacpp`, `vllm`, and `ollama` server kinds to their documented request constraint fields.\n- Derive constraints from the current allowlisted tool schemas; preserve the existing request when provider kind is unknown or the endpoint/protocol does not support the field.\n- Files: `cli/src/core/tool_grammar_provider.ts` (new), `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts` (new).\n- Tests: AC1–AC4 request-shape tests above.\n- References to retrieve: official llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama server request implementations/docs.\n\n**Chunk 2 — Client integration**\n\n- Attach the adapter output to non-streaming, streaming, and supported native client request bodies without changing message, tool, credential, or egress handling.\n- Files: `cli/src/client.ts`, `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts`.\n- Tests: captured request-body tests for each applicable transport path, plus AC4 fallback.\n- Reference to retrieve: `LocalLlmClient` request construction and provider identification on the exact candidate.\n\n**Chunk 3 — Candidate endpoint matrix**\n\n- Add a deterministic runner and schema validator. Fake-provider/serialization tests validate request construction and evidence validation only; they do not count as live endpoint measurements.\n- Files: `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.mjs` (new), `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.test.mjs` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json.sha256` (new detached digest), `docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md`.\n- Tests: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`.\n- Matrix: run the exact installed archive against one allowlisted llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama endpoint. Before the run, the matrix must pin each server to an immutable image/version and record model name plus immutable model digest, context size, sampling settings, tool choice, prompt fixture digest, server flags, and relevant compatibility mode. Record provider identification from the detected `serverKind` returned by the client; the lane is valid only when that value exactly matches the configured provider (`llamacpp`, `vllm`, or `ollama`). Unknown/mismatched identities fail before attempts and receive no constraint fields.\n- Evidence fields: archive SHA-256 and source commit; provider/server kind; server version and immutable image digest; model name and immutable model digest; non-secret configuration and stimulus digest; total attempts and per-tool attempts; native-valid/repaired/invalid counts per endpoint; exact pass equation result; evidence schema version. Store the finalized evidence file’s SHA-256 in the detached `.sha256` sidecar and qualification record (the JSON cannot hash itself). Omit endpoint URLs, credentials, and other secrets.\n- Test/evidence: AC5 threshold and zero-invalid requirement for every provider. This measures argument JSON only; it does not certify tool selection or task correctness.\n- Dependency: explicit release of the shared `GATEWAY` claim before any inference.\n\n## Risks and reference queries\n\n- Provider request contracts may have changed since the roadmap draft. Retrieve current primary references before implementation:\n  - `llama.cpp` (`https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp`) — chat-completion grammar request field and interaction with function tools.\n  - `vLLM` (`https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm`) — current structured-output request field and function-tool compatibility.\n  - `Ollama` (`https://github.com/ollama/ollama`) — current chat `format` schema and function-tool compatibility.\n  - `nexus-cli` XERJ prefix — exact candidate request construction and provider identification.\n- A provider field that constrains the whole response may conflict with native function calling; retain only combinations shown to be supported by the primary references and captured-request tests.\n- G1.1-4 requires a real endpoint matrix. A loopback mock proves serialization only and cannot satisfy the 99.5% metric.\n- The provider lane’s immutable server and model identities are not yet confirmed because the shared `GATEWAY` claim is still held by the RC15 stream. Treat them as a pre-run gate: record and peer-review the three exact identities/configurations in the matrix plan before starting any provider call; do not substitute mutable tags or infer identity from an endpoint URL.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":419659594}},"started_at_ms":1793735605613,"completed_at_ms":1793735607033}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:53:28.681Z","ordinal":79582,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1a2f9-4969-7822-9bdb-d98cca4e36c1","call_id":"call_92479b3292aa47aeaf33904a4d0416a3","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 4.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"f486fc\",\"wall_time_seconds\":1.3134434050000001,\"exit_code\":2,\"original_token_count\":34,\"output\":\"sed: can't read .entire/agent-guide.md: No such file or directory\\nsed: can't read docs/v1.1.0/CONCURRENCY.md: No such file or directory\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"f5859a\",\"wall_time_seconds\":1.420219675,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1881,\"output\":\" docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md | 11 ++++++-----\\n 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)\\n# WP-1107 — Provider-Constrained Tool Grammar\\n\\n## Specification\\n\\n**User story:** As a Nexus user running a supported local inference engine, I need registered tool arguments constrained by that engine so tool calls arrive as valid JSON and require fewer syntax repairs.\\n\\n**Acceptance criteria:**\\n\\n- **AC1:** An identified llama.cpp chat endpoint receives the grammar generated from the active allowlisted tool schemas. Test: `client sends llama.cpp GBNF for supported tool calls`.\\n- **AC2:** An identified vLLM endpoint receives the provider's supported JSON-schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends vLLM structured tool schema for supported tool calls`.\\n- **AC3:** An identified Ollama endpoint receives its supported schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends Ollama JSON schema format for supported tool calls`.\\n- **AC4:** Unknown and unsupported endpoints retain their current request shape; no grammar field is sent without positive provider identification. Test: `client omits constrained fields for unknown and unsupported providers`.\\n- **AC5:** On each supported endpoint, the exact installed candidate completes 200 attempts: 50 for each of four fixed allowlisted tool schemas. Each attempt requests exactly one tool call. `native_valid` means the raw provider argument string parses with `JSON.parse` before any normalization or repair; `repaired` means raw parsing fails and the existing tool-call repair path produces parseable JSON; `invalid` includes every other outcome, including provider errors, timeouts, missing calls, and unparseable arguments. An endpoint passes only when `native_valid * 1000 >= attempts * 995` and `invalid == 0`; report exact integer counts and do not round percentages for the decision. Tests/evidence: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`; live `WP-1107 supported-endpoint syntax matrix`.\\n\\n**Out of scope:** Changing tool schemas or the allowlist; applying constraints to non-tool completions; measuring task-solving quality; enabling constraints on an endpoint that does not positively identify itself.\\n\\n**Dependencies:** Existing schema-derived grammar generator and `toolCallSyntax` telemetry; source implementation is otherwise independent. AC5 depends on an approved shared inference window and the exact candidate package.\\n\\n**Scope reconciliation:** The user selected the full failed v1.1.0 WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap for v1.1.2. This explicit scope supersedes the older deferral in `ROADMAP-REVIEW.md` §2.5. It does not waive the required provider references, test gates, review, or G1.1-4 endpoint evidence.\\n\\n## Implementation plan\\n\\n**Chunk 1 — Provider request adapter**\\n\\n- Add a typed adapter mapping positively identified `llamacpp`, `vllm`, and `ollama` server kinds to their documented request constraint fields.\\n- Derive constraints from the current allowlisted tool schemas; preserve the existing request when provider kind is unknown or the endpoint/protocol does not support the field.\\n- Files: `cli/src/core/tool_grammar_provider.ts` (new), `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts` (new).\\n- Tests: AC1–AC4 request-shape tests above.\\n- References to retrieve: official llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama server request implementations/docs.\\n\\n**Chunk 2 — Client integration**\\n\\n- Attach the adapter output to non-streaming, streaming, and supported native client request bodies without changing message, tool, credential, or egress handling.\\n- Files: `cli/src/client.ts`, `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts`.\\n- Tests: captured request-body tests for each applicable transport path, plus AC4 fallback.\\n- Reference to retrieve: `LocalLlmClient` request construction and provider identification on the exact candidate.\\n\\n**Chunk 3 — Candidate endpoint matrix**\\n\\n- Add a deterministic runner and schema validator. Fake-provider/serialization tests validate request construction and evidence validation only; they do not count as live endpoint measurements.\\n- Files: `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.mjs` (new), `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.test.mjs` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json.sha256` (new detached digest), `docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md`.\\n- Tests: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`.\\n- Matrix: run the exact installed archive against one allowlisted llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama endpoint. Before the run, the matrix must pin each server to an immutable image/version and record model name plus immutable model digest, context size, sampling settings, tool choice, prompt fixture digest, server flags, and relevant compatibility mode. Record provider identification from the detected `serverKind` returned by the client; the lane is valid only when that value exactly matches the configured provider (`llamacpp`, `vllm`, or `ollama`). Unknown/mismatched identities fail before attempts and receive no constraint fields.\\n- Evidence fields: archive SHA-256 and source commit; provider/server kind; server version and immutable image digest; model name and immutable model digest; non-secret configuration and stimulus digest; total attempts and per-tool attempts; native-valid/repaired/invalid counts per endpoint; exact pass equation result; evidence schema version. Store the finalized evidence file’s SHA-256 in the detached `.sha256` sidecar and qualification record (the JSON cannot hash itself). Omit endpoint URLs, credentials, and other secrets.\\n- Test/evidence: AC5 threshold and zero-invalid requirement for every provider. This measures argument JSON only; it does not certify tool selection or task correctness.\\n- Dependency: explicit release of the shared `GATEWAY` claim before any inference.\\n\\n## Risks and reference queries\\n\\n- Provider request contracts may have changed since the roadmap draft. Retrieve current primary references before implementation:\\n  - `llama.cpp` (`https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp`) — chat-completion grammar request field and interaction with function tools.\\n  - `vLLM` (`https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm`) — current structured-output request field and function-tool compatibility.\\n  - `Ollama` (`https://github.com/ollama/ollama`) — current chat `format` schema and function-tool compatibility.\\n  - `nexus-cli` XERJ prefix — exact candidate request construction and provider identification.\\n- A provider field that constrains the whole response may conflict with native function calling; retain only combinations shown to be supported by the primary references and captured-request tests.\\n- G1.1-4 requires a real endpoint matrix. A loopback mock proves serialization only and cannot satisfy the 99.5% metric.\\n- The provider lane’s immutable server and model identities are not yet confirmed because the shared `GATEWAY` claim is still held by the RC15 stream. Treat them as a pre-run gate: record and peer-review the three exact identities/configurations in the matrix plan before starting any provider call; do not substitute mutable tags or infer identity from an endpoint URL.\\n\"}}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","create_time":1793735608.6812983}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
