{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T20:03:52.920Z","ordinal":80205,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0fd4138577b17921016aea3e25e9f481a3a1a64c3958388644","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"The llama.cpp source clarifies the boundary: it builds its own tool-call grammar from the submitted function schemas, while a caller-supplied JSON response schema takes precedence and changes the output parser. That means I can’t simply send the generated argument grammar alongside native tools. I’m preserving the native tool contract and checking whether vLLM and Ollama expose compatible per-tool paths before revising the plan."}],"phase":"commentary"},"started_at_ms":1793736229965,"completed_at_ms":1793736232920}}
