{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T20:12:46.423Z","ordinal":80735,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a1a30a-f3d7-7e72-be2d-dbfd12c54048","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Current: 2026-11-03 8:12pm UTC\nThis file has prior observations — supplementary context follows. The Read result below is the full requested section.\n- **Need details on a past observation?** get_observations([IDs]) — ~300 tokens each.\n- **Need a structural map first?** smart_outline(\"docs/v1.1.0/WP-1107-PLAN.md\") — line numbers only, cheaper than re-reading.\n### Nov 3, 2026\n29326 7:55p ✅ WP-1107-PLAN.md AC4 Expanded with Provider Identity Handshake Tests"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","create_time":1793736766.4230716,"content_item_kinds":["hooks.additional_context"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"harness","hook":true}}]}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T20:12:47.323Z","ordinal":80736,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-5f780d83-2ffe-4fe5-b390-dc3e5094c383","process_id":"69149","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","cat docs/v1.1.0/WP-1107-PLAN.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1101-integration","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat docs/v1.1.0/WP-1107-PLAN.md","name":"WP-1107-PLAN.md","path":"docs/v1.1.0/WP-1107-PLAN.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# WP-1107 — Provider-Constrained Tool Grammar\n\n## Specification\n\n**User story:** As a Nexus user running a supported local inference engine, I need registered tool arguments constrained by that engine so tool calls arrive as valid JSON and require fewer syntax repairs.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n\n- **AC1:** An identified llama.cpp chat endpoint receives the grammar generated from the active allowlisted tool schemas. Test: `client sends llama.cpp GBNF for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC2:** An identified vLLM endpoint receives the provider's supported JSON-schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends vLLM structured tool schema for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC3:** An identified Ollama endpoint receives its supported schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends Ollama JSON schema format for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC4:** Unknown, unsupported, or ambiguously identified endpoints retain their current request shape; no grammar field is sent based only on a configured lane label or generic OpenAI-compatible catalog fields. Tests: `client omits constrained fields for unknown and unsupported providers`; `generic context catalog remains unknown and receives no grammar field`; `provider identity accepts llama.cpp only with its marker and valid props handshake`; `provider identity accepts vLLM only with its documented version response`; `provider identity accepts Ollama only with version and selected-model show response`; `client omits constrained fields for missing malformed or mismatched provider identity`.\n- **AC5:** On each supported endpoint, the exact installed candidate completes 200 attempts: 50 for each of four fixed allowlisted tool schemas. Each attempt requests exactly one tool call. `native_valid` means the raw provider argument string parses with `JSON.parse` before any normalization or repair; `repaired` means raw parsing fails and the existing tool-call repair path produces parseable JSON; `invalid` includes every other outcome, including provider errors, timeouts, missing calls, and unparseable arguments. An endpoint passes only when `native_valid * 1000 >= attempts * 995` and `invalid == 0`; report exact integer counts and do not round percentages for the decision. Tests/evidence: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`; live `WP-1107 supported-endpoint syntax matrix`.\n\n**Out of scope:** Changing tool schemas or the allowlist; applying constraints to non-tool completions; measuring task-solving quality; enabling constraints on an endpoint that does not positively identify itself.\n\n**Dependencies:** Existing schema-derived grammar generator and `toolCallSyntax` telemetry; source implementation is otherwise independent. AC5 depends on an approved shared inference window and the exact candidate package.\n\n**Scope reconciliation:** The user selected the full failed v1.1.0 WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap for v1.1.2. This explicit scope supersedes the older deferral in `ROADMAP-REVIEW.md` §2.5. It does not waive the required provider references, test gates, review, or G1.1-4 endpoint evidence.\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n**Chunk 1 — Provider request adapter**\n\n- Add a typed adapter mapping provider kinds to documented request constraint fields only after a provider-specific positive identity check. A configured lane label or `context_length`/`max_model_len` alone is insufficient.\n- Derive constraints from the current allowlisted tool schemas; preserve the existing request when provider kind is unknown or the endpoint/protocol does not support the field.\n- Files: `cli/src/core/tool_grammar_provider.ts` (new), `cli/src/core/provider_capabilities.ts`, `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts` (new), `cli/test/provider_capabilities.test.ts`.\n- Tests: AC1–AC4 request-shape and identity tests above, including positive provider-handshake fixtures and generic/missing/malformed/mismatched negative cases.\n- Identity contract: llama.cpp requires the exact `/v1/models` `owned_by: \"llamacpp\"` marker or its `meta.n_ctx_train` marker plus a successful `/props` response matching the existing llama.cpp schema; Ollama requires its `/api/version` response with a non-empty `version` string and a successful `/api/show` response for the selected model; vLLM requires a successful `GET /version` returning the provider's documented JSON version response and the selected model in `/v1/models`. A generic catalog containing only `context_length` or `max_model_len` remains `unknown` for grammar attachment, even if the matrix lane is configured as vLLM. These are provider self-identification handshakes on the operator-configured endpoint, not cryptographic server authentication; any mismatch, missing probe, or malformed response disables constrained fields.\n- References to retrieve: official llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama server request implementations/docs.\n\n**Chunk 2 — Client integration**\n\n- Attach the adapter output to non-streaming, streaming, and supported native client request bodies without changing message, tool, credential, or egress handling.\n- Files: `cli/src/client.ts`, `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts`.\n- Tests: captured request-body tests for each applicable transport path, plus AC4 fallback.\n- Reference to retrieve: `LocalLlmClient` request construction and provider identification on the exact candidate.\n\n**Chunk 3 — Candidate endpoint matrix**\n\n- Add a deterministic runner and schema validator. Fake-provider/serialization tests validate request construction and evidence validation only; they do not count as live endpoint measurements.\n- Files: `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.mjs` (new), `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.test.mjs` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json.sha256` (new detached digest), `docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md`.\n- Tests: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`.\n- Matrix: run the exact installed archive against one allowlisted llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama endpoint. Before the run, the matrix must pin each server to an immutable image/version and record model name plus immutable model digest, context size, sampling settings, tool choice, prompt fixture digest, server flags, and relevant compatibility mode. Record provider identification from the detected `serverKind` returned by the client; the lane is valid only when that value exactly matches the configured provider (`llamacpp`, `vllm`, or `ollama`). Unknown/mismatched identities fail before attempts and receive no constraint fields.\n- Evidence fields: archive SHA-256 and source commit; provider/server kind; server version and immutable image digest; model name and immutable model digest; non-secret configuration and stimulus digest; total attempts and per-tool attempts; native-valid/repaired/invalid counts per endpoint; exact pass equation result; evidence schema version. Store the finalized evidence file’s SHA-256 in the detached `.sha256` sidecar and qualification record (the JSON cannot hash itself). Omit endpoint URLs, credentials, and other secrets.\n- Test/evidence: AC5 threshold and zero-invalid requirement for every provider. This measures argument JSON only; it does not certify tool selection or task correctness.\n- Dependency: explicit release of the shared `GATEWAY` claim before any inference.\n\n## Risks and reference queries\n\n- Provider request contracts may have changed since the roadmap draft. Retrieve current primary references before implementation:\n  - `llama.cpp` (`https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp`) — chat-completion grammar request field and interaction with function tools.\n  - `vLLM` (`https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm`) — current structured-output request field and function-tool compatibility.\n  - `Ollama` (`https://github.com/ollama/ollama`) — current chat `format` schema and function-tool compatibility.\n  - `nexus-cli` XERJ prefix — exact candidate request construction and provider identification.\n- A provider field that constrains the whole response may conflict with native function calling; retain only combinations shown to be supported by the primary references and captured-request tests.\n- G1.1-4 requires a real endpoint matrix. A loopback mock proves serialization only and cannot satisfy the 99.5% metric.\n- The provider lane’s immutable server and model identities are not yet confirmed because the shared `GATEWAY` claim is still held by the RC15 stream. Treat them as a pre-run gate: record and peer-review the three exact identities/configurations in the matrix plan before starting any provider call; do not substitute mutable tags or infer identity from an endpoint URL.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":724560943}},"started_at_ms":1793736766598,"completed_at_ms":1793736767323}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-03T20:12:49.170Z","ordinal":80737,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1a30a-fe91-7e82-ab39-3cafcda11070","call_id":"call_c40c9e2193724d519ae852efebfc862e","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 4.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# WP-1107 — Provider-Constrained Tool Grammar\n\n## Specification\n\n**User story:** As a Nexus user running a supported local inference engine, I need registered tool arguments constrained by that engine so tool calls arrive as valid JSON and require fewer syntax repairs.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n\n- **AC1:** An identified llama.cpp chat endpoint receives the grammar generated from the active allowlisted tool schemas. Test: `client sends llama.cpp GBNF for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC2:** An identified vLLM endpoint receives the provider's supported JSON-schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends vLLM structured tool schema for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC3:** An identified Ollama endpoint receives its supported schema-constrained tool-call request shape. Test: `client sends Ollama JSON schema format for supported tool calls`.\n- **AC4:** Unknown, unsupported, or ambiguously identified endpoints retain their current request shape; no grammar field is sent based only on a configured lane label or generic OpenAI-compatible catalog fields. Tests: `client omits constrained fields for unknown and unsupported providers`; `generic context catalog remains unknown and receives no grammar field`; `provider identity accepts llama.cpp only with its marker and valid props handshake`; `provider identity accepts vLLM only with its documented version response`; `provider identity accepts Ollama only with version and selected-model show response`; `client omits constrained fields for missing malformed or mismatched provider identity`.\n- **AC5:** On each supported endpoint, the exact installed candidate completes 200 attempts: 50 for each of four fixed allowlisted tool schemas. Each attempt requests exactly one tool call. `native_valid` means the raw provider argument string parses with `JSON.parse` before any normalization or repair; `repaired` means raw parsing fails and the existing tool-call repair path produces parseable JSON; `invalid` includes every other outcome, including provider errors, timeouts, missing calls, and unparseable arguments. An endpoint passes only when `native_valid * 1000 >= attempts * 995` and `invalid == 0`; report exact integer counts and do not round percentages for the decision. Tests/evidence: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`; live `WP-1107 supported-endpoint syntax matrix`.\n\n**Out of scope:** Changing tool schemas or the allowlist; applying constraints to non-tool completions; measuring task-solving quality; enabling constraints on an endpoint that does not positively identify itself.\n\n**Dependencies:** Existing schema-derived grammar generator and `toolCallSyntax` telemetry; source implementation is otherwise independent. AC5 depends on an approved shared inference window and the exact candidate package.\n\n**Scope reconciliation:** The user selected the full failed v1.1.0 WP-1101–WP-1108 roadmap for v1.1.2. This explicit scope supersedes the older deferral in `ROADMAP-REVIEW.md` §2.5. It does not waive the required provider references, test gates, review, or G1.1-4 endpoint evidence.\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n**Chunk 1 — Provider request adapter**\n\n- Add a typed adapter mapping provider kinds to documented request constraint fields only after a provider-specific positive identity check. A configured lane label or `context_length`/`max_model_len` alone is insufficient.\n- Derive constraints from the current allowlisted tool schemas; preserve the existing request when provider kind is unknown or the endpoint/protocol does not support the field.\n- Files: `cli/src/core/tool_grammar_provider.ts` (new), `cli/src/core/provider_capabilities.ts`, `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts` (new), `cli/test/provider_capabilities.test.ts`.\n- Tests: AC1–AC4 request-shape and identity tests above, including positive provider-handshake fixtures and generic/missing/malformed/mismatched negative cases.\n- Identity contract: llama.cpp requires the exact `/v1/models` `owned_by: \"llamacpp\"` marker or its `meta.n_ctx_train` marker plus a successful `/props` response matching the existing llama.cpp schema; Ollama requires its `/api/version` response with a non-empty `version` string and a successful `/api/show` response for the selected model; vLLM requires a successful `GET /version` returning the provider's documented JSON version response and the selected model in `/v1/models`. A generic catalog containing only `context_length` or `max_model_len` remains `unknown` for grammar attachment, even if the matrix lane is configured as vLLM. These are provider self-identification handshakes on the operator-configured endpoint, not cryptographic server authentication; any mismatch, missing probe, or malformed response disables constrained fields.\n- References to retrieve: official llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama server request implementations/docs.\n\n**Chunk 2 — Client integration**\n\n- Attach the adapter output to non-streaming, streaming, and supported native client request bodies without changing message, tool, credential, or egress handling.\n- Files: `cli/src/client.ts`, `cli/test/client_tool_grammar.test.ts`.\n- Tests: captured request-body tests for each applicable transport path, plus AC4 fallback.\n- Reference to retrieve: `LocalLlmClient` request construction and provider identification on the exact candidate.\n\n**Chunk 3 — Candidate endpoint matrix**\n\n- Add a deterministic runner and schema validator. Fake-provider/serialization tests validate request construction and evidence validation only; they do not count as live endpoint measurements.\n- Files: `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.mjs` (new), `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.test.mjs` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json` (new), `docs/evidence/v1.1.2/wp-1107-provider-matrix.json.sha256` (new detached digest), `docs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md`.\n- Tests: `tool grammar matrix evidence schema rejects invalid counts and identity omissions`; `tool grammar matrix computes endpoint pass without percentage rounding`; `tool grammar matrix runner records archive and per-endpoint identity`.\n- Matrix: run the exact installed archive against one allowlisted llama.cpp, vLLM, and Ollama endpoint. Before the run, the matrix must pin each server to an immutable image/version and record model name plus immutable model digest, context size, sampling settings, tool choice, prompt fixture digest, server flags, and relevant compatibility mode. Record provider identification from the detected `serverKind` returned by the client; the lane is valid only when that value exactly matches the configured provider (`llamacpp`, `vllm`, or `ollama`). Unknown/mismatched identities fail before attempts and receive no constraint fields.\n- Evidence fields: archive SHA-256 and source commit; provider/server kind; server version and immutable image digest; model name and immutable model digest; non-secret configuration and stimulus digest; total attempts and per-tool attempts; native-valid/repaired/invalid counts per endpoint; exact pass equation result; evidence schema version. Store the finalized evidence file’s SHA-256 in the detached `.sha256` sidecar and qualification record (the JSON cannot hash itself). Omit endpoint URLs, credentials, and other secrets.\n- Test/evidence: AC5 threshold and zero-invalid requirement for every provider. This measures argument JSON only; it does not certify tool selection or task correctness.\n- Dependency: explicit release of the shared `GATEWAY` claim before any inference.\n\n## Risks and reference queries\n\n- Provider request contracts may have changed since the roadmap draft. Retrieve current primary references before implementation:\n  - `llama.cpp` (`https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp`) — chat-completion grammar request field and interaction with function tools.\n  - `vLLM` (`https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm`) — current structured-output request field and function-tool compatibility.\n  - `Ollama` (`https://github.com/ollama/ollama`) — current chat `format` schema and function-tool compatibility.\n  - `nexus-cli` XERJ prefix — exact candidate request construction and provider identification.\n- A provider field that constrains the whole response may conflict with native function calling; retain only combinations shown to be supported by the primary references and captured-request tests.\n- G1.1-4 requires a real endpoint matrix. A loopback mock proves serialization only and cannot satisfy the 99.5% metric.\n- The provider lane’s immutable server and model identities are not yet confirmed because the shared `GATEWAY` claim is still held by the RC15 stream. Treat them as a pre-run gate: record and peer-review the three exact identities/configurations in the matrix plan before starting any provider call; do not substitute mutable tags or infer identity from an endpoint URL.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19de1-2f43-7663-a2d9-f17adf8cb101","create_time":1793736769.1699615}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
